diagnosis_text:
No active outage is present at Location C. All four PCs in that site (pc11–pc14, 192.168.200.11–.14) have correct addressing and default routes pointing to the site gateway 192.168.200.1. From each PC, ping to the gateway succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs (e.g., pc11→192.168.200.1: 3/3 received, ~0.83 ms). Hosts within Location C can reach each other (pc11↔pc13/pc14 OK), and cross‑site connectivity is working (pc11→pc4 at 172.16.10.4: 4/4 received, ~2 ms).

The gateway router (router4) is healthy: interface eth0 is up with 192.168.200.1/24, and its routing table includes a default via 172.25.1.2 on eth5 plus RIP-learned routes for other sites. The upstream router (router3) reciprocally has a route to 192.168.200.0/24 via 172.25.1.1, confirming bidirectional reachability at the routing layer.

Mechanistically, if Location C were cut off or its gateway unreachable, we would observe failed pings from the PCs to 192.168.200.1 and/or missing/incorrect routes on router4; neither is true. Localization checks on the site LAN and the R4–R3 uplink show normal operation.

Root cause: No persistent network fault could be reproduced; the reported issue appears to have cleared or was unrelated to network forwarding (e.g., transient or endpoint/DNS issue).

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the problem recurs, please capture a timestamp and test ping to 192.168.200.1 and an offsite IP; we can enable additional monitoring on router4’s LAN and uplink if needed.
